<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Khazneh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as The Treasury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Khazneh, 'the Treasury', is a monumental facade carved into the sandstone cliff at the end of the Siq gorge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, Jordan. Built probably in the 1st century CE, perhaps as the tomb of the Nabataean king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a>, it combines Hellenistic columns and sculpture with Nabataean features. Local legend held that a treasure was hidden in the urn at its top.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khazneh.jpg" alt="Al-Khazneh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Susanahajer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_khazneh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Al-Khazneh, also known as Khaznat el-Far'oun, the treasury of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, is one of the most elaborate rock-cut tombs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, a city of the Nabatean Kingdom inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in ancient times. Like most of the buildings there, including the Monastery, Ad Deir, it was carved out of a sandstone rock face. It is thought to have been built as a mausoleum and crypt at the beginning of the 1st century CE, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV Philopatris</a>.</p><p>The name Al-Khazneh, Arabic for the Treasury, derives from legends about the decorative stone urn high on the second level, which is in reality solid sandstone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouins</a> began calling it Al-Khazneh in the early nineteenth century because they believed it held treasure. One legend says the Egyptian pharaoh, escaping the closing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> while pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, created it by magic as a safe place for his treasury. Significant bullet damage can be seen on the urn, which the Jordanian government attributes to Bedouins who believed the legend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Many of the architectural details have eroded over two millennia. The sculptures are thought to represent mythological figures associated with the afterlife. On top are four eagles that would carry away souls, the figures on the upper level are dancing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a> with double axes, and the entrance is flanked by the twins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>, who lived partly on Olympus and partly in the underworld. In contrast to the elaborate façade, the interior consists of a plain main chamber and three antechambers, with an interior volume of around 2,000 cubic metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Swiss explorer Burckhardt noted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> and Al-Khazneh in 1812. As exploration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> continued, tourism became more common, and by the 1920s a small hotel had opened near Petra. Humidity from crowds and stearic acid from hands resting on the walls have caused damage, and the surface of the Khazneh receded by 40 mm in less than ten years. The monument gained particular fame as the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>'s resting place in the 1989 film Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade. The Zamani Project documented it in 2012 by laser scanning, making a permanent record for research and conservation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Khazneh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its end stands <b>Al-Khazneh</b>, the Treasury, 24 metres wide and 37 metres tall, its façade carved with a broken pediment around a central tholos, the twins Castor and Pollux, and a female figure believed to be Isis-Tyche.</blockquote><a href="/work/petra">Petra · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Petra's facades, such as <b>Al-Khazneh</b>, mix Greek, Egyptian and local styles.</blockquote><a href="/state/nabataean-kingdom">Nabataean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
